\details{
Use it when the robots question is "would \emph{this engine} fetch these URLs".
\code{\link[=validate_sitemap]{validate_sitemap()}} carries only \code{robots_user_agent}, a matcher user-agent
string that widens onto the Google policy and matcher; this entry point
carries all three axes, so the \code{ROBOTS_DISALLOWED} /
\code{ROBOTS_INDETERMINATE} / \code{ROBOTS_SITEMAP_DISALLOWED} findings are decided by
the selected engine's own status policy and matcher. Under the default
\code{robots_context()} the result is exactly \code{validate_sitemap(check_robots = TRUE)}, plus the added column.

The robots axes are independent of the \code{sitemap_ruleset} axis (ADR-009 §1):
this entry point selects an engine's \strong{robots} semantics and does not
select a sitemap ruleset. It returns the baseline schema-v1 result, so the
additive per-engine ruleset columns of \code{\link[=validate_sitemap_ruleset]{validate_sitemap_ruleset()}} are not
present. To select both axes, call \code{\link[=validate_sitemap_ruleset]{validate_sitemap_ruleset()}} with its
\code{robots_context} argument; independence means the axes are chosen separately,
not that they cannot be chosen together. This entry point remains the
shorthand for the robots axis alone, and is exactly
\code{validate_sitemap_ruleset(x, "sitemaps.org", robots_context = context)}.

A backend the installed \code{robotstxtr} reports as \code{capability_unavailable}
decides nothing rather than guessing: every advertised URL comes back as
\code{ROBOTS_INDETERMINATE}. The same is true of a product token a bounded
matcher backend does not accept — prefer \code{\link[=robots_context_preset]{robots_context_preset()}}, whose
tokens are known-good for their backend.
}
